Her mother tried to pull her down. She wouldn’t come.
    The attendants had stopped moving in step.
    They stood along the wall in their gray polos, in a crooked line, with
their hands loose at their sides and their earbuds still in. The click was
still in their ears. But the horns were in the room, and the horns had no
grid. Each player entered where there was a hole. I watched an attend‐
ant’s head tilt, and his foot lift on the one, and stay in the air, unde‐
cided.
    The ring on the platform was coming apart.
    I could see it. Forty people in a circle, hands in their laps, a hum go‐
ing up from them like the note of a wine glass. But the hum had a new
thing in it now, a roughness, a grain. A woman near the rail lifted her
chin. A man’s knee jumped. The boy of twelve, with his big dark eyes
and his hands clamped between his knees, was looking at the horns
with his mouth open as if somebody had said his name.
    Vale had come down from the lectern.
    He didn’t hurry. He crossed the carpet with his hands folded, in that
calm, unbroken stride, toward the loading doors, toward me and
Manny and the case, toward a procession of people who, on paper, had
a slot at the end of the evening. He stopped three feet from the old wo‐
man with the long horn.
    “Madame,” he said, warmly. “You’re early.”
    She lowered the horn.
    He let a pause open.
    I counted. One. Two.
    At three, the boy in the red shirt blew a note.
    Four: the second horn. Five: a drum. Six: the old woman put the
horn to her lips again, and looked at Vale, and played a long, slightly
flat, completely unapologetic A.
    Every one of his eight-second silences had been built for a nervous
person to fill. Tonight the room had come with plenty to say.
    The silence filled up.
    Vale stood there with his mouth slightly open and his pen hand
hanging at his side.



